{-
   Sorting a container whose elements own storage.

   `sort` never needed anything of the element but `Ord`: every movement it makes is a `swap` of two
   subscripts, no pivot is lifted into a temporary, and Hoare's partition is chosen over Lomuto's
   precisely so that the pivot can be compared in place. What it did anyway was corrupt the
   container, and the reason was neither the algorithm nor the constraint.

   Those movements went through a `swapElements` that both platform files carried, because
   `swap(xs[i], xs[j])` did not compile. Its native body was `swap(*(p + i), *(p + j))`, and each
   argument is resolved as an expression before the borrow is taken out of its place - so each
   element was *loaded* into a temporary, that temporary was an ordinary owned local, and the drop
   placer released it at its last use while the memory it came out of still held the same value. The
   swap then exchanged two freed buffers. A read out of a raw pointer is `outsideModel` on the
   handover side and had no matching statement on the destination side; it does now, and the
   temporary is no longer droppable.

   `swapElements` is gone with it - the subscript form compiles on both platforms - so what this
   fixture guards is the rule rather than the one body that used to depend on it.

   It is invisible for every element whose teardown is nothing, which is the whole reason it lasted:
   the constraint on `sorted` was `TrivialCopy(a)`, so the only element types that reached the sort
   through the library were exactly the ones that could not show it.

   `sorted` and `sortedBy` ask `Copy(a)` rather than `TrivialCopy(a)` for a separate reason - see
   Sort.yana - and this is what says the pair of changes works together rather than only compiling.
-}

fn named(n: Int) -> String = "item" ++ ("{n}" :: String)

fn built() -> Array(String):
    let &out = [] :: [String]
    push(out, named(3))
    push(out, named(1))
    push(out, named(2))
    return out

fn sortInPlace() -> {}:
    let &xs = built()
    sort(xs)

    assert(length(xs) == 3)
    assert(xs[0] == "item1")
    assert(xs[1] == "item2")
    assert(xs[2] == "item3")

-- The copying form, which is what the constraint change makes reachable for an owning element.
fn sortedCopies() -> {}:
    let &xs = built()
    let &out = sorted(xs)

    assert(out[0] == "item1")
    assert(out[2] == "item3")

    -- And the argument is left as it was, which is the whole of what `sorted` promises over `sort`.
    assert(xs[0] == "item3")
    assert(xs[1] == "item1")

fn longerThanInsertion() -> {}:
    let &xs = [] :: [String]
    let &i = 0
    while i < 40:
        push(xs, named((i * 17) % 40))
        i = i + 1

    sort(xs)

    assert(length(xs) == 40)
    assert(xs[0] == "item0")

    -- Every neighbour in order, which is what catches a partition that lost an element rather than
    -- one that misplaced it.
    let &j = 1
    while j < 40:
        assert(xs[j - 1] <= xs[j])
        j = j + 1

fn main() -> Int:
    sortInPlace()
    sortedCopies()
    longerThanInsertion()
    return 0
